WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:04.240
Before we get started,
I have a confession.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.080
This is my first attempt at a sock

3
00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:12.480
in the round. I've really never
understood the appeal. Seaming up a sock

4
00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:14.480
is it a problem for me.

5
00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:19.360
But I get it, lots of knitters like
to avoid having a seam in their socks.

6
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:20.880
OK.

7
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:23.200
Like every first attempt,

8
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:29.000
I made every mistake in the book,
but I hope the lessons I learned will help

9
00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.800
you avoid these mistakes when
you try a seamless sock.

10
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:39.680
This is my preferred method, and I'll
offer a few options for you to try.

11
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:44.680
The basis of this sock is tubular
knitting with the ribber.

12
00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:49.240
Before you start knitting,
be sure that you can knit in the round.

13
00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:53.880
Check your manual and do a little
test knitting before starting.

14
00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:59.320
I was so sure that I knew the carriage
settings on this machine.

15
00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.640
But it took me three toes and three
tries before I got it right.

16
00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:11.440
So before you start knitting, please do
a little test swatch of circular knitting.

17
00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:13.280
Here's the goal,

18
00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:18.800
we're going to combine hand and Machine
knitting for a seamless sock.

19
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.280
Starting with a Short row toe,
I'll switch to knitting in round

20
00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:27.760
with my ribber.
I'll add some provisional stitches

21
00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:32.120
for an after-thought heel
and then scrap off in the round.

22
00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:37.040
I'll pick up the cuff and knit in
ribbing on double-point needles by hand.

23
00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:41.920
Then I'll use those same hand knitting
needles and knit the heel by hand.

24
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:43.080
Let's get started.

25
00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.440
I'm going to assume that you
know how to short row a toe.

26
00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.520
Here is my green crochet cast-on rag

27
00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:56.720
and a row of blue ravel cord
and I've knit one row of my sock.

28
00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.920
I'm using my single bed carriage.

29
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:05.960
I'll start working my short rows
to form the heel. Short row decrease.

30
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.680
Then Short row increase.

31
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:18.720
And here's my little toe pouch.

32
00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:23.400
These live stitches are going
to end up on the ribber.

33
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:24.920
I don't know about you,

34
00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.960
but the thought of raising the Ribber
right now and hanging those live

35
00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:33.240
stitches on the ribber needles,
it doesn't sound like fun to me.

36
00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.080
So instead, I'm going to hang those live

37
00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:40.480
stitches on the main bed,
doubling up the stitches for now.

38
00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.520
When all of the stitches are
on the needles, I can remove the ravel

39
00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.680
cord and the waste yarn or
in my case, my cast-on rag..

40
00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:52.280
I'm going to be knitting
in the round with my ribber.

41
00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:58.560
As you know, we need lots of even weight
when we're knitting on the ribber.

42
00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:01.120
I'm using this triangle weight hanger.

43
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:04.160
You could hang a regular Ribber Cast-on

44
00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:06.840
comb and just poke it right
through the knitting.

45
00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:08.800
You have to play with this a bit,

46
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:14.520
but make sure that you have good
weight on both pieces of the knitting.

47
00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:17.760
Next, I'll swap out the main bed pressure

48
00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:21.680
plate assembly for the
Ribber connecting arm.

49
00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:25.040
With the weight in place and the stitches

50
00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:28.240
secure,
I'll raise my Ribber.

51
00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:34.320
And rack it to "P" so the needles are
directly across from each other.

52
00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:35.720
Wait, wait, wait.

53
00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.680
This is only temporary when we start
knitting, the Ribber needs to be racked

54
00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:44.720
to "H" or those needles are
going to crash into each other.

55
00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:49.520
Guess how many times it took
me to remember that one?

56
00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.400
Here's the fun part,
transfer that second set of stitches

57
00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.560
from the main bed needles down
to the ribber.

58
00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:03.400
I tried a bunch of different methods for
transferring those individual stitches.

59
00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:07.440
Use whatever method works for you.

60
00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:10.080
Notice how having the Ribber needle's

61
00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:15.760
aligned with the main bed needles
makes it easier to transfer.

62
00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:20.480
This whole process seems tedious,
but there really aren't that many stitches

63
00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:27.040
and repeat after me,
no seam ...No seam ...No seam.

64
00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:31.200
As I mentioned before,
this seems easier to me than trying

65
00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:35.920
to pick up stitches from the scrap and
hanging them up directly on the ribber.

66
00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:38.680
You might disagree.

67
00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:40.680
Once all the stitches are on

68
00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:46.280
the ribber, take time to put all
the stitches on both beds in the hooks

69
00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:51.400
of the needles, align the needles.
Make everything neat and tidy.

70
00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:55.080
Trust me, take time to do this.

71
00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.680
And don't forget to rack to "H"

72
00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:06.040
the needles need to alternate so they knit
and they don't crash into each other.

73
00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:10.840
Back to the weight issue,
you'll probably want to use wire weight

74
00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:18.040
hangers on the edges so the edge stitches
knit properly as you knit in the round.

75
00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:24.160
Whew... after hitting the toe and taking
time to transfer the stitches twice.

76
00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:29.880
I hope you practiced tubular knitting
and you don't have to start 3 times

77
00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:36.560
like I did. With the Ribber racked to "H"
the carriages joined and threaded

78
00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:41.080
Knit in one direction on the main bed
and slip on the ribber.

79
00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:46.440
Then knit on the ribber and slip
on the main bed. Knitting in the round.

80
00:05:46.640 --> 00:05:54.080
And do this for the length of the foot
of the sock all the way to the heel.

81
00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:58.240
Now, here's where you have some options.

82
00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:01.440
Theoretically, you could short row shape

83
00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:06.600
the heel of the sock now on the main bed.
You could leave the Ribber stitches

84
00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:11.880
in place, set your Ribber to slip
and shape a heel on the main bed.

85
00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:14.040
Stitches only.

86
00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:20.880
If you've ever knit a Short row heel or
toe, you know that it takes a lot

87
00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:28.000
of weight and those stitches tend to
misbehave as the short row pouch grows.

88
00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.040
If you have kept the stitches
on the ribber,

89
00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:36.280
you'll need to reach up underneath
and hang lots of weight or stand on your

90
00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:40.000
head to make sure that the
stitches knit correctly.

91
00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:42.720
I took the easier way out.

92
00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:45.200
When it came time to knit the heel,

93
00:06:45.400 --> 00:06:50.640
I manually knit a provisional
row with Ravel cord.

94
00:06:57.320 --> 00:06:59.000
I didn't use all of the needles

95
00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:04.040
on the main bed, I just left
a couple of needles on each side.

96
00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:09.320
And then I used some clips to secure
the ends of the Ravel cord.

97
00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:12.840
With that provisional row in place,

98
00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:17.560
I continued knitting in the round
right over those stitches.

99
00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:19.560
To finish the foot of my sock,

100
00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:26.200
I switched to some scrap yarn and then
scraped off by knitting in the round.

101
00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:32.000
Voila,
a short row toe and a tube with the blue

102
00:07:32.200 --> 00:07:39.120
provisional row ready for the after-
thought heel and live stitches.

103
00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:41.600
I went outside, turned on some music,

104
00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:46.880
poured a glass of wine and picked up
those live stitches on my double points.

105
00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:52.400
I removed the scrap yarn and
knit a little ribbing.

106
00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:57.720
The blue provisional stitches are
sitting there waiting for heel.

107
00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:00.560
Again, I picked up the live stitches,

108
00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:05.400
removed the ravel cord
and hand knit the heel.

109
00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:08.480
OK, it's an ugly pointed heel.

110
00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:10.840
Blame it on the wine.

111
00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:13.480
Could I have hung those provisional

112
00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:18.520
stitches on the machine and Short
row shaped the heel on the machine?

113
00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:20.280
Sure.

114
00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:24.760
But as I mentioned before,
I'm not a fan of struggling.

115
00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:26.480
How about you?

116
00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:30.560
OK, it's really an ugly sock,

117
00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:36.160
but there are no seams,
and hopefully it will inspire you to try

118
00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:39.640
adding circular knitting
to your sock knitting skills.

